2. Preliminaries and Notations
2.1. Genomic Sequence Modeling
DNA is a polymer made of up four types of nucleotides: Adenine ( A ), Thymine ( T ), Guanine ( G ), and Cytosine ( C ). Let N 4 = { A,T,G,C } . A DNA sequence of length T , denoted as x = ( x 1 , x 2 , ..., x T ) ∈ N T 4 , follows a natural distribution x ∼ p θ ( x ) . We use p ˆ θ ( x ) to represent an estimate to the true distribution. The dataset of unlabeled genomic sequences is given in the form of { x ( i ) } N i =1 .
Genomic sequence modeling aims to learn a function f that maps input sequences to biological annotations using a labeled dataset D = { x ( i ) , y ( i ) } N i =1 . The type of y ( i ) varies depending on the types of tasks: y ( i ) is a class label in DNA Sequence Classification (Greˇ sov´ a et al., 2023; Dalla-Torre et al., 2024). Or a real value vector in Genomic Assay Prediction tasks (Avsec et al., 2021a; Linder et al., 2025). Current genomic sequence models typically follow a two-stage strategy. In the pretraining phase, we learn the data distribution p ˆ θ ( x ) on a unlabeled dataset from unlabeled data using losses such as masked language modeling (MLM) p ( x ) = ∏ t ∈M p θ ( x t | x 1: t -1 , x t +1: T ) or next token prediction (NTP) p ( x ) = ∏ T t =1 p θ ( x t | x 1: t -1 ) .
